  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "decoupling-interpretation",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Decoupling results: how to read them\n",
    "\n",
    "- A country is labeled **absolutely decoupled** here only when its GDP increased and its total CO₂ emissions decreased between the comparison years. The scatter plot makes this group visible in the lower-right quadrant; the printed count uses only countries with data at both endpoints.\n",
    "- The ranked table and bar chart show the largest percentage decreases in CO₂ among those countries. The case-study chart shows annual emissions for three of these countries and three countries that did not meet the definition; these are selected examples, not a representative sample.\n",
    "- The Welch t-test compares mean percentage changes in CO₂ per unit of GDP for the decoupled and other groups. Read its p-value as an association in this dataset, not evidence that decoupling caused intensity to change. The groups are defined using GDP and total CO₂ changes, and CO₂ per GDP is mathematically related to both, so the test is circular and should not be treated as an independent confirmation.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Key findings\n",
    "\n",
    "Complete these three statements from the printed results and charts after running the analysis cell:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Of **[eligible country count]** countries with endpoint data, **[decoupled count]** had GDP growth while total CO₂ fell (**[percent]%**).\n",
    "2. **[country or countries]** had the largest CO₂ percentage reductions among the decouplers, while their GDP changed by **[percent]%**.\n",
    "3. In the case-study chart, **[describe the annual trend and any rebound or sustained decline]**; the scatter plot and line chart together show why endpoints alone do not describe the path year by year.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "- Requiring complete data at both endpoints can bias the sample toward wealthier, better-documented countries; countries omitted for missing data may have different outcomes.\n",
    "- The t-test has the circularity described above and does not establish causation. Treat it as exploratory, not as a standalone statistical conclusion.\n",
    "- The results depend on the chosen years and the CO₂ and GDP measures in this dataset; they do not capture every greenhouse gas or explain why emissions changed."
   ]
  }
